meta {
  name: Sign in
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{email}}",
    "password": "{{password}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  bru.deleteVar("token");
  if (res.status === 200 && res.body?.token) {
    bru.setVar("token", res.body.token);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  # Sign in

  `POST /api/auth/login`

  Authenticate an active account, update lastLoginAt, and issue an 8-hour HS256 JWT.

  ## Authentication

  Public; no Authorization header.

  ## Parameters

  No path or query parameters.

  ## Request body

  | Field | Type | Rules |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | email | string | Required, not normalized during login. |
  | password | string | Required, checked using BCrypt. |

  Send Content-Type: application/json.

  ```json
  {
    "email": "{{email}}",
    "password": "{{password}}"
  }
  ```

  ## Response

  HTTP **200**.

  ```json
  {
    "token": "eyJ...example-token",
    "username": "Admin",
    "role": "Admin"
  }
  ```

  ## Errors

  - 400: malformed JSON or model validation.
  - 401: Invalid credentials. / Account is deactivated. Please contact an administrator.

  Automatic model validation can return ValidationProblemDetails; explicit controller errors use a message field. Authentication middleware errors may have no body.

  ## Notes

  Set email and secret password in the selected Local or Docker environment. On success the post-response script stores token as a runtime variable. No refresh token is issued. Email comparison is exact.

  Examples contain fictitious data. The assertion checks the successful scenario; a missing record or insufficient permissions can produce a different documented status.
}
